\label{quesentgro}
Assume $|B_n|  \geq K_1\mathrm{exp}(K_2n^v)$, does
\begin{enumerate}
 \item $H(P^n) \geq K n^{v/2}$?
 \item $H(P^n) \geq K n^{v/(1+v)}$?
 \end{enumerate}

\label{exzz}
Consider the lazy simple random walk on $\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}} \wr \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}$ with respect to the standard generators (add $\pm 1$ to the lamp at the position of the lamplighter, or move the lamplighter), and write $X_n = (\varphi_n, x_n)$. Then $H(P^n) \asymp \sqrt n \ln n$ (see Erschler \cite{Ers-drift}), so that the increments of the entropy are of order $\ln n/\sqrt n$ by concavity, as in Example \ref{exz2z2}; the speed is $L(n) \asymp n^{3/4}$ (Erschler \cite{Dyu}, Revelle \cite{Revelle}); and the F\o lner function is $\asymp k^k$ (Erschler \cite{Ers-isop}), \emph{i.e.}  $\mathcal{G}^{\scalebox{.4}{$\searrow$}}(x) \asymp \ln\ln x/\ln x$. Hence Proposition \ref{ptvspeed} gives $\mathrm{TV}_n \succeq n^{-3/4}$, Proposition \ref{pentprofile}(a) and the quotient $\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}$ (Lemma \ref{lquot}) give $\mathrm{TV}_n \succeq n^{-1/2}$, and Proposition \ref{prevpinsker} gives $\mathrm{TV}_n \succeq \ln n/\sqrt n$.
The truth is larger. Left multiplication by a lamp generator adds $1$ to the lamp at the origin, which is a simple random walk run for a number of steps of order $\sqrt n$; as the total variation of a sequence $k \mapsto \mu(k)$ is at least $2\max_k \mu(k)$, projecting on this lamp gives $\mathrm{TV}_n \geq \mathbbold{P}\big(\varphi_n(0) = 0\big) \asymp n^{-1/4}$. Together with the entropy-increment inequality, $n^{-1/4} \preceq \mathrm{TV}_n \preceq n^{-1/4}\sqrt{\ln n}$: although the growth is exponential, the upper end of $H(P^{n+1}) - H(P^n) \preceq \mathrm{TV}_n \preceq \big(H(P^{n+1}) - H(P^n)\big)^{1/2}$ is attained, the discrepancy being a small displacement of a lamp value rather than a rare event.

\label{rrenyismallp}
For $0 < p < 1$ the functions $H_p(P^n)$ are probably equivalent to $H_0(P^n) = \ln |B_n|$ on groups of polynomial or of exponential growth, but not on groups of intermediate growth. Recall that R\'enyi entropies are non-increasing in $p$, so that $H_1 \leq H_p \leq H_0$ for $0<p<1$.
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\bullet$] For a group of polynomial growth of degree $d$, the two-sided Gaussian estimates (Hebisch \& Saloff-Coste \cite{HSC}) give $H_p(P^n) = \tfrac d2 \ln n + O(1)$ for every $p > 0$, whereas $H_0(P^n) \asymp d \ln n$; these are equivalent.
\item[$\bullet$] For a group of exponential growth $H_0(P^n) \asymp n$, and the sum $\sum_x P^n(x)^p$ is dominated by large deviations: the points at distance $\rho n$ carry a total probability of order $e^{-c\rho^2 n}$, but they are exponentially numerous and $x \mapsto x^p$ favours small values. On the lamplighter $(\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}/2\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}) \wr \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}$ this heuristic can be checked: forcing the lamplighter to travel a distance $\rho n$ costs a probability of order $e^{-c\rho^2 n}$ and leaves the lamps essentially uniform on about $\rho n$ sites, which suggests $\sum_x P^n(x)^p \geq \mathrm{exp}\big(n[(1-p)\rho \ln 2 - pc\rho^2]\big)$, and choosing $\rho$ small gives $H_p(P^n) \succeq n$ for every $p < 1$.
\item[$\bullet$] If $|B_r| \leq \mathrm{exp}(Cr^V)$ with $V < 1$, the estimate of Varopoulos \& Carne, $P^n(x) \leq 2e^{-|x|^2/2n}$ \cite{Var,Carne}, and H\"older's inequality on spheres give $H_p(P^n) \leq C_p\, n^{V/(2-V)}$ for $0 < p < 1$. This is $o(n^V)$, so if moreover $|B_r| \geq \mathrm{exp}(cr^V)$, then $H_p(P^n)$ is \emph{not} equivalent to $H_0(P^n) \asymp n^V$. The same heuristic suggests that $H_p(P^n) \asymp n^{V/(2-V)}$ for every $p \in \, ]0,1[$ in that case.
\end{itemize}
In all three cases the range $0<p<1$ would then consist of a single equivalence class, determined by the volume growth and by the Gaussian decay of $P^n$ rather than by the typical behaviour of the walk; it coincides with the class of $H_0$ exactly when the growth is polynomial or exponential.
